rrect for the line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\). However, substituting \\(x=n\\) into (1) gives\n\\[\ny=\\frac{1}{1-n}\\,n+\\frac{n}{n-1}= -\\frac{n}{n-1}+\\frac{n}{n-1}=0,\n\\]\nso the line passes through \\((n,0)\\), **not** \\((n,1)\\). The correct intercept should be \\(\\frac{2n-1}{n-1}\\). Hence the line claimed in the solution does **not** cover the uncovered point \\((n,1)\\).  \n\nBecause the construction fails to cover all points of \\(S_n\\), the existence proof for \\(k=1\\) is invalid.  \n**Issue:** **Critical Error** – the line does not satisfy the required property.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Setup for the impossibility proof (\\(k\\ge2\\))\n\n> “Assume that a configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) sunny lines exists.  \n> Let the set of non‑sunny lines consist of  \n> * \\(H\\) horizontal lines \\(y=b\\) (\\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)),  \n> * \\(V\\) vertical lines \\(x=a\\) (\\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\)),  \n> * \\(D\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=c\\) (\\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\)).  \n> … Because the total number of non‑sunny lines equals \\(n-k\\),  \n> \\[\n> |H|+|V|+|D| = n-k .\\tag{2}\n> \\]”\n\n**Verification:**  \nThe classification of non‑sunny lines is exhaustive and mutually exclusive. Equation (2) correctly records that the number of non‑sunny lines is \\(n-k\\).  \n**Conclusion:** Correct.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Description of uncovered points\n\n> “A point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) that is **not** covered by a non‑sunny line must satisfy  \n> \\[\n> a\\notin V,\\qquad b\\notin H,\\qquad a+b\\notin D .\\tag{3}\n> \\]  \n> Hence the set of uncovered points is contained in  \n> \\[\n> U:=\\bigl\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\in V^{c},\\;b\\in H^{c},\\;a+b\\in D^{c}\\bigr\\}.\n> \\]”\n\n**Verification:**  \nThe logical negation of “covered by a non‑sunny line” is precisely that the point lies on none of the horizontal, vertical, or diagonal lines present. The notation \\(V^{c}, H^{c}, D^{c}\\) denotes the complements within the respective index sets \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\). This description is accurate.  \n**Conclusion:** Correct.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Cardinalities of the complements\n\n> “The complementary sets have cardinalities  \n> \\[\n> |V^{c}| = n-|V|,\\qquad |H^{c}| = n-|H|,\\qquad |D^{c}| = (n-1)-|D|.\n> \\]”\n\n**Verification:**  \nThe first two equalities are correct because the possible vertical (resp. horizontal) indices are \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nFor diagonal lines, the possible values of \\(c\\) are \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\), which contains **\\(n\\)** elements, not \\(n-1\\). Therefore the correct size of the complement is \\(|D^{c}| = n - |D|\\). The statement \\((n-1)-|D|\\) is a factual mistake.  \n\n**Issue:** **Critical Error** – the count of diagonal complements is wrong. Although this number is not used later in the argument, the mistake signals a lack of careful bookkeeping and contributes to later incorrect bounds.\n\n---\n\n### 7. Upper bound for \\(|U|\\)\n\n> “For any fixed pair \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\in V^{c},\\,b\\in H^{c}\\) there is at most one value of \\(c\\) for which \\(a+b=c\\). Consequently  \n> \\[\n> |U|\\le (n-|V|)(n-|H|). \\tag{4}\n> \\]”\n\n**Verification:**  \nGiven \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), the sum \\(a+b\\) is uniquely determined, so at most one diagonal index can be missing. Hence each admissible pair \\((a,b)\\) contributes at most one uncovered point. The product \\((n-|V|)(n-|H|)\\) counts all such admissible pairs, so the inequality (4) is valid.  \n**Conclusion:** Correct.\n\n---\n\n### 8. Derivation of a further bound on \\(|U|\\)\n\n> “From (2) we have \\(|V|+|H|\\ge n-k-|D|\\ge n-k-(n-1)=1-k\\).  \n> Since \\(k\\ge 2\\), at least one of \\(|V|,|H|\\) is \\(\\le n-1\\); therefore  \n> \\[\n> (n-|V|)(n-|H|)\\le (n-1)^{2}. \\tag{5}\n> \\]  \n> Thus  \n> \\[\n> |U|\\le (n-1)^{2}. \\tag{6}\n> \\]”\n\n**Verification:**  \nThe first inequality \\(|V|+|H|\\ge n-k-|D|\\) follows from (2). However, the subsequent estimate \\(|D|\\le n-1\\) is **incorrect** (the correct bound is \\(|D|\\le n\\)). Consequently the derived lower bound \\(|V|+|H|\\ge 1-k\\) is not guaranteed.  \n\nEven assuming the (incorrect) bound \\(|V|+|H|\\ge 1-k\\), the conclusion that “at least one of \\(|V|,|H|\\) is \\(\\le n-1\\)” is trivial (both are always \\(\\le n\\)). The claimed inequality (5) does **not** follow: for many admissible pairs \\((|V|,|H|)\\) the product \\((n-|V|)(n-|H|)\\) can be as large as \\(n^{2}\\) (e.g., \\(|V|=|H|=0\\)). Hence (5) and (6) are **false**.  \n\n**Issue:** **Critical Error** – the bound \\(|U|\\le (n-1)^{2}\\) is not justified and is in fact false in general.\n\n---\n\n### 9. Claim about the covering capacity of a sunny line\n\n> “A sunny line is not parallel to any of the three families, so it meets each horizontal line, each vertical line and each diagonal line in at most one point. Hence a sunny line can contain **at most three** points of the set \\(U\\) (one from each family).”\n\n**Verification:**  \nThe definition of \\(U\\) is the set of points **not** lying on any of the horizontal, vertical, or diagonal lines present. The fact that a sunny line intersects each *family* at most once does **not** limit the number of points of \\(U\\) it can contain, because points of \\(U\\) are precisely those that avoid all those families. A sunny line may pass through many such points without ever intersecting a horizontal, vertical, or diagonal line. Therefore the “three‑point” claim is false.  \n\n**Issue:** **Critical Error** – the argument that each sunny line can cover at most three points of \\(U\\) is incorrect.\n\n---\n\n### 10. Consequence for the number of points covered by sunny lines\n\n> “Thus the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(3k\\) points of \\(U\\).  \n> All points of \\(S_n\\) must be covered, so  \n> \\[\n> 3k\\ge |U|\\ge |S_n|-(n-k)n .\n> \\]”\n\n**Verification:**  \nThe inequality \\(3k\\ge |U|\\) rests on the false “three‑point” bound from the previous step, so it is unsupported. The lower bound \\(|U|\\ge |S_n|-(n-k)n\\) is correct (each non‑sunny line can cover at most \\(n\\) points), but the upper bound is not. Consequently the derived inequality (7) is not valid.  \n\n**Issue:** **Critical Error** – the inequality is based on a false premise.\n\n---\n\n### 11. Derivation of inequality (7) and (8)\n\n> “Hence  \n> \\[\n> 3k\\ge\\frac{n(2k-n+1)}2 .\\tag{7}\n> \\]  \n> Multiplying (7) by \\(2\\) and rearranging gives  \n> \\[\n> (6-n)k\\ge n(1-n). \\tag{8}\n> \\]”\n\n**Verification:**  \nSince inequality (7) itself is unfounded, the algebraic manipulation that follows does not rescue the argument. The conclusion that \\(k\\le1\\) is therefore not justified.  \n\n**Issue:** **Critical Error** – the conclusion relies on the previously invalid bound.\n\n---\n\n### 12. Small‑case verification for \\(n=3,4,5\\)\n\n> “For \\(n=3\\)… exhaustive enumeration … confirms that the maximal number of sunny lines that can be accommodated is one.  \n> For \\(n=4\\)… a direct case‑by‑case analysis … confirms that the maximal number of sunny lines that can be accommodated is one.  \n> For \\(n=5\\)… a computer‑assisted search verifies that no configuration with two sunny lines exists.”\n\n**Verification:**  \nThe author asserts that exhaustive or computer‑assisted searches have been performed, but provides no data, algorithmic description, or verification of completeness. As a result, the claim is **unsubstantiated** within the written solution.  \n\n**Issue:** **Justification Gap** – the small‑case verification is asserted without proof.\n\n---\n\n### 13. Final conclusion\n\n> “Thus the admissible values of \\(k\\) are \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=1\\) for all \\(n\\ge 3\\).”\n\n**Verification:**  \nBecause the existence proof for \\(k=1\\) is flawed and the impossibility proof for \\(k\\ge2\\) contains multiple critical errors, the conclusion is not logically supported.  \n\n**Overall Assessment:** The solution fails to provide a rigorous proof of the claimed result. The critical errors identified break the logical chain, rendering the answer invalid."
