+j\\le m,\\qquad a+(a+j)\\le m+1,\n   \\]\n   i.e. \\(1\\le a\\le m-|j|\\).  Hence each \\(L_j\\) contributes the whole \u201c\\(j\\)-th diagonal\u2019\u2019 of the triangle, and the family \\(\\{L_j\\}\\) covers all of \\(\\mathcal P_m\\).\n\n   *Lemma 3 (full construction).*  \n   Let \\(k\\) be admissible.  \n   - Take the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines\n     \\[\n     y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k .\n     \\]\n     They are non\u2011sunny and together cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with second coordinate \\(\\le n-k\\).  \n   - The still uncovered points are precisely the translate\n     \\[\n     \\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le k,\\; n-k+1\\le b\\le n,\\; a+b\\le n+1\\}\n     =\\{(a,b')\\mid (a,b')\\in\\mathcal P_k\\},\n     \\]\n     i.e. a copy of \\(\\mathcal P_k\\).  \n   - If \\(k=0\\) we are done.  If \\(k\\ge1\\) (and \\(k\\) is odd by Lemma\u202f1) we apply Lemma\u202f2 to this copy of \\(\\mathcal P_k\\); after translating the lines back by adding \\(n-k\\) to the \\(y\\)-coordinate we obtain \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering the remaining points.\n\n   Thus for every admissible \\(k\\) we have built \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones that cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n4. **Impossibility of the excluded values**  \n   *Lemma 4.*  If \\(k\\) is even and \\(k>0\\) then no configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.  \n   *Proof.*  Remove the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  As in Lemma\u202f1 the uncovered set is a lattice triangle of side\u2011length \\(k\\).  Any sunny line can intersect this triangle in at most two of its lattice points (otherwise two points would share the same row, column or diagonal, forcing the line to be parallel to one of the prohibited directions).  Hence at most \\(2k\\) points can be covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines, but the triangle contains \\(k(k+1)/2\\) points, which exceeds \\(2k\\) for every even \\(k\\ge2\\).  Contradiction.  \n\n   The case \\(k=n\\) with even \\(n\\) is a 