ne label of each kind for each of its points, we necessarily have  \n\n\\[\ns\\le \\min\\{\\,n-h,\\;n-v,\\;n-d\\,\\}\\le n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nMoreover, from (2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(n-h)+(n-v)+(n-d)=3n-(n-k)=2n+k .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe left\u2011hand side of (4) is the sum of three integers each congruent to \\(n\\) modulo \\(2\\); therefore the sum is congruent to \\(k\\) modulo \\(2\\).  Consequently the three numbers \\(n-h,\\;n-v,\\;n-d\\) have the **same parity as \\(k\\)**.  Because \\(s\\) does not exceed any of them, \\(s\\) must also have the same parity as \\(k\\).\n\nThus we have proved:\n\n> **Lemma 1.**  \n> Let \\(\\mathcal U\\) be the set of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that remain uncovered after all non\u2011sunny lines are removed.  Then \\(\\mathcal U\\) is tri\u2011distinct, \\(|\\mathcal U|\\le n\\), and \\(|\\mathcal U|\\equiv k\\pmod 2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A sunny line meets a tri\u2011distinct set in at most one point\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line and let \\(P,Q\\in\\mathcal U\\cap L\\) be two distinct points.  \nIf \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) had the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, then the vertical line through them would be parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, i.e. non\u2011sunny, contradicting the definition of a sunny line.  The same argument rules out equality of the \\(y\\)-coordinates or of the sums \\(x+y\\).  Hence a sunny line cannot contain two points of a tri\u2011distinct set.\n\n> **Lemma 2.**  \n> Every sunny line contains **at most one** point of \\(\\mathcal U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Necessity of the parity condition\n\nBecause the \\(k\\) sunny lines must cover all points of \\(\\mathcal U\\), Lemma\u202f2 gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge |\\mathcal U|.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nTogether with Lemma\u202f1 we obtain  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge |\\mathcal U|\\le n,\\qquad |\\mathcal U|\\equiv k\\pmod 2 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(k>0\\) were even, then \\(|\\mathcal U|\\) would be an even positive integer not exceeding \\(k\\).  Inequality (5) would then force \\(|\\mathcal U|=k\\).  But Lemma\u202f2 says that each sunny line can cover **only one** point of \\(\\mathcal U\\); consequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines would have to be pairwise distinct and would each cover a different point of \\(\\mathcal U\\).  In that situation the remaining \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines would have to cover **all** other points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  However the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines can delete at most \\(n-k\\) labels of each kind, leaving at least \\(k+1\\) available labels of one kind (by the pigeon\u2011hole principle).  Hence any tri\u2011distinct set has size at most \\(k\\), contradicting the fact that \\(|\\mathcal U|=k\\).  The contradiction shows that an even \\(k>0\\) cannot occur.\n\nThus we have proved:\n\n> **Lemma 3 (Parity Lemma).**  \n> If a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists, then either \\(k=0\\) or \\(k\\) is odd.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be either \\(0\\) or an odd integer with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWrite \\(k=2t+1\\) (\\(t\\ge 0\\)).\n\n#### 5.1  Non\u2011sunny part  \n\nTake the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_i:\\; y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k\\) lines are non\u2011sunny, distinct, and together cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose second coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n#### 5.2  The remaining points  \n\nThe points still uncovered after (6) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R_k=\n\\bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\;\\big|\\;\n1\\le a\\le k,\\;\nn-k+1\\le b\\le n\n\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting \\((0,n-k)\\) from the second coordinate maps \\(\\mathcal R_k\\) bijectively onto \\(\\mathcal P_k\\); consequently \\(\\mathcal R_k\\) is a right\u2011isosceles lattice triangle of side\u2011length \\(k\\).\n\n#### 5.3  Sunny covering of \\(\\mathcal R_k\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(-t\\le j\\le t\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; y = x + j + (n-k).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(S_j\\) have slope \\(1\\); therefore they are sunny (the prohibited slopes are \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n\nFix \\(j\\).  A point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal R_k\\) lies on \\(S_j\\) iff  \n\n\\[\nb = a + j + (n-k).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal R_k\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le k\\) and \\(n-k+1\\le b\\le n\\); the last inequality is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le k-|j|.\n\\]\n\nThus the points of \\(\\mathcal R_k\\) on \\(S_j\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\, (a,\\;a+j+(n-k))\\mid a=1,2,\\dots ,k-|j| \\,\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(j\\) runs through \\(-t,-t+1,\\dots ,t\\) the sets (9) are exactly the \u201c\\(j\\)-th diagonal\u2019\u2019 of the triangle \\(\\mathcal R_k\\); they are pairwise disjoint and their union is \\(\\mathcal R_k\\).  Hence the family \\(\\{S_j\\}_{j=-t}^{t}\\) covers **all** points of \\(\\mathcal R_k\\).\n\n#### 5.4  Counting  \n\nThe construction uses the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines (6) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines (8).  All lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines.  Therefore a configuration satisfying the statement exists for every admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Completion of the proof\n\n* Lemma\u202f3 shows that no even \\(k>0\\) can occur.  \n* Lemma\u202f5 (the construction of Section\u202f5) shows that every odd \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) and also \\(k=0\\) can be realised.\n\nHence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\;\\text{or}\\;k\\text{ odd with }1\\le k\\le n\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3,5,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,3,5,\\dots ,n\\},   & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
