der the \\(k\\) lines\n   \\[\n   S_j:\\; y = x + j + (n-k),\\qquad j=-t,-t+1,\\dots ,t .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Each \\(S_j\\) has slope \\(1\\); therefore it is sunny (slope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n   For a fixed \\(j\\) the integer points of \\(\\mathcal R_k\\) lying on \\(S_j\\) are precisely\n   \\[\n   \\{(a,\\,a+j+(n-k))\\mid 1\\le a\\le k-|j|\\}.\n   \\]\n   The condition \\(a+(a+j+(n-k))\\le n+1\\) is equivalent to \\(a\\le k-|j|\\), so the description is correct.  Hence \\(S_j\\) contains exactly the “\\(j\\)-th diagonal’’ of the triangle \\(\\mathcal R_k\\).  As \\(j\\) runs from \\(-t\\) to \\(t\\) these diagonals partition \\(\\mathcal R_k\\); consequently the family \\(\\{S_j\\}_{j=-t}^{t}\\) covers **all** points of \\(\\mathcal R_k\\).\n\n   *Step 4 – counting.*  \n   The construction uses \\(n-k\\) horizontal (non‑sunny) lines (1) and \\(k\\) sunny lines (3); all lines are distinct.  Every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one of them, and exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.  Thus for every admissible \\(k\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n7. **Conclusion** – Lemma 5 (parity) shows that no even \\(k>0\\) can occur; Lemma 6 (the explicit construction) shows that every admissible odd \\(k\\) and \\(k=0\\) can be realised.  This yields the complete description stated in the answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nN_n:=|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non‑sunny**.  \nThe three families of non‑sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal V=\\{x=c\\},\\qquad \n\\mathcal D=\\{x+y=c\\},\n\\]\nwhere \\(c\\) is an integer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Structure of the uncovered set\n\nAssume that we have \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \nDelete all non‑sunny lines.  Let \\(\\mathcal U\\) be the set of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that are **not** covered by the remaining \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nIf a point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal U\\) shared its \\(x\\)-coordinate with another point of \\(\\mathcal U\\), then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) would contain two points of \\(\\mathcal U\\); that vertical line is non‑sunny, contradicting the fact that we have removed all non‑sunny lines.  The same argument applies to the \\(y\\)-coordinate and to the sum \\(a+b\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{All points of }\\mathcal U\\text{ have pairwise distinct }x\\text{-, }y\\text{- and }(x+y)\\text{-coordinates.}}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA set satisfying (1) is called **tri‑distinct**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Size of a tri‑distinct set\n\nLet a tri‑distinct set contain \\(s\\) points.  Associate to each point the three labels \\(x\\), \\(y\\) and \\(x+y\\).  Because the labels are all distinct, the set uses exactly \\(s\\) different \\(x\\)-labels, \\(s\\) different \\(y\\)-labels and \\(s\\) different sum‑labels.\n\nWhen we removed the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, we deleted exactly one label of one of the three kinds for each removed line.  Hence the total number of deleted labels is \\(n-k\\).  Denote by  \n\n\\[\nh,\\;v,\\;d\\qquad (h+v+d=n-k)\n\\]\n\nthe numbers of deleted horizontal, vertical and diagonal labels, respectively.  After the deletion the numbers of *available* labels of the three kinds are  \n\n\\[\nn-h,\\qquad n-v,\\qquad n-d .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSince a tri‑distinct set must choose one label of each kind for each of its points, we necessarily have  \n\n\\[\ns\\le \\min\\{\\,n-h,\\;n-v,\\;n-d\\,\\}\\le n .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nMoreover, from (2) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n(n-h)+(n-v)+(n-d)=3n-(n-k)=2n+k .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe left‑hand side of (4) is