ver a different point of \\(\\mathcal U\\).  In that situation the remaining \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines would have to cover **all** other points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  However the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines can delete at most \\(n-k\\) labels of each kind, leaving at least \\(k+1\\) available labels of one kind (by the pigeon‑hole principle).  Hence any tri‑distinct set has size at most \\(k\\), contradicting the fact that \\(|\\mathcal U|=k\\).  The contradiction shows that an even \\(k>0\\) cannot occur.\n\nThus we have proved:\n\n> **Lemma 3 (Parity Lemma).**  \n> If a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists, then either \\(k=0\\) or \\(k\\) is odd.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be either \\(0\\) or an odd integer with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWrite \\(k=2t+1\\) (\\(t\\ge 0\\)).\n\n#### 5.1  Non‑sunny part  \n\nTake the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_i:\\; y=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny, distinct, and together cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose second coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\n\n#### 5.2  The remaining points  \n\nThe points still uncovered after (6) are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R_k=\n\\bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\;\\big|\\;\n1\\le a\\le k,\\;\nn-k+1\\le b\\le n\n\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSubtracting \\((0,n-k)\\) from the second coordinate maps \\(\\mathcal R_k\\) bijectively onto \\(\\mathcal P_k\\); consequently \\(\\mathcal R_k\\) is a right‑isosceles lattice triangle of side‑length \\(k\\).\n\n#### 5.3  Sunny covering of \\(\\mathcal R_k\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(-t\\le j\\le t\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; y = x + j + (n-k).\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(S_j\\) have slope \\(1\\); therefore they are sunny (the prohibited slopes are \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n\nFix \\(j\\).  A point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal R_k\\) lies on \\(S_j\\) iff  \n\n\\[\nb = a + j + (n-k).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal R_k\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le k\\) and \\(n-k+1\\le b\\le n\\); the last inequality is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le k-|j|.\n\\]\n\nThus the points of \\(\\mathcal R_k\\) on \\(S_j\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\, (a,\\;a+j+(n-k))\\mid a=1,2,\\dots ,k-|j| \\,\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(j\\) runs through \\(-t,-t+1,\\dots ,t\\) the sets (9) are exactly the “\\(j\\)-th diagonal’’ of the triangle \\(\\mathcal R_k\\); they are pairwise disjoint and their union is \\(\\mathcal R_k\\).  Hence the family \\(\\{S_j\\}_{j=-t}^{t}\\) covers **all** points of \\(\\mathcal R_k\\).\n\n#### 5.4  Counting  \n\nThe construction uses the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines (6) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines (8).  All lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) belongs to at least one of the lines.  Therefore a configuration satisfying the statement exists for every admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Completion of the proof\n\n* Lemma 3 shows that no even \\(k>0\\) can occur.  \n* Lemma 5 (the construction of Section 5) shows that every odd \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n\\) and also \\(k=0\\) can be realised.\n\nHence the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\;\\text{or}\\;k\\text{ odd with }1\\le k\\le n\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3,5,\\dots ,n-1\\}, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,3,5,\\dots ,n\\},   & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n∎"
