le a\\le k,\\;\n          s+1\\le b\\le n+1-a\n   \\Bigr\\}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed, the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) together with \\(b\\ge s+1\\) forces\n   \\(a\\le k\\); conversely any pair \\((a,b)\\) satisfying (2) belongs to\n   \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and has \\(b>s\\), so it is not hit by any \\(H_i\\).\n   The set \\(\\mathcal R_k\\) is a right‑isosceles lattice triangle whose side\n   length is \\(k\\); consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal R_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Sunny covering of \\(\\mathcal R_k\\).**  \n   For each integer \\(j\\) with \\(0\\le j\\le k-1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_j:\\; y = x + (s+1)-j .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of every \\(S_j\\) equals \\(1\\); because \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), each\n   \\(S_j\\) is sunny.\n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal R_k\\).  From (2) we have \\(s+1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\),\n   whence  \n\n   \\[\n   b-a\\in\\{\\,s+1-(k-1),\\,s+1-(k-2),\\dots ,s+1\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Thus there exists a unique \\(j\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) such that\n   \\(b-a = (s+1)-j\\); equivalently \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of\n   \\(S_j\\).  Hence each point of \\(\\mathcal R_k\\) lies on exactly one of the\n   lines \\(S_0,\\dots ,S_{k-1}\\), and the family \\(\\{S_j\\}_{j=0}^{k-1}\\)\n   covers the whole set \\(\\mathcal R_k\\).\n\n4. **Counting and distinctness.**  \n   The total number of lines used is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{s}_{\\text{horizontal}}+\\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny}}=\n   (n-k)+k=n .\n   \\]\n\n   The horizontal lines have distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates, while the sunny lines\n   have distinct constants \\((s+1)-j\\); therefore all \\(n\\) lines are\n   distinct.  Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_j\\) are sunny.\n\nThus for the given \\(k\\) we have produced a configuration satisfying the\nrequirements. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2 (Necessity)\n\nIf a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the covering condition\nand contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, then \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe number of sunny lines cannot be negative, and there are only \\(n\\) lines\nin total, so \\(k\\le n\\).  No further restriction is needed because Lemma 1\nshows that every integer in this interval can actually be realised. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nCombining Lemma 1 (existence) with Lemma 2 (obvious bound) we obtain that,\nfor a given integer \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{the admissible values of }k\\text{ are precisely }0,1,2,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]\n\nThat is, every non‑negative integer not exceeding \\(n\\) can occur as the\nnumber of sunny lines in a suitable configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines\ncovering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
