ing \\(\\mathcal R_k\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines.*  \n   For each integer \\(j\\) with \\(0\\le j\\le k-1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   S_j:\\; y = (j+1)\\,x + \\bigl(s+1-(j+1)\\bigr).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(S_j\\) equals \\(j+1\\); because \\(j+1\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\),\n   each \\(S_j\\) is sunny.\n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal R_k\\).  Since \\(1\\le a\\le k\\), write \\(j:=a-1\\)\n   (so \\(0\\le j\\le k-1\\)).  Substituting \\(j\\) into (6) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   y = a\\,x + (s+1-a).\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Evaluating (7) at \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y = a^2 + s+1-a\\).  Because\n   \\(b\\) satisfies \\(s+1\\le b\\le n+1-a = s+k+1-a\\), we have  \n\n   \\[\n   b = a + (s+1-a) = s+1,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. the point \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(S_{a-1}\\).  Conversely,\n   any point of \\(S_{a-1}\\) with integer coordinates and with\n   \\(1\\le a\\le k\\) automatically satisfies the bounds defining\n   \\(\\mathcal R_k\\).  Hence each point of \\(\\mathcal R_k\\) belongs to exactly\n   one of the lines \\(S_0,\\dots ,S_{k-1}\\).\n\n   *Step 4 – assembling the whole family.*  \n   The total collection consists of the \\(s=n-k\\) horizontal lines (3) and the\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines (6); all are distinct because they have different\n   \\(y\\)-intercepts (for the horizontals) or different slopes (for the sunny\n   lines).  Exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and together they contain every\n   point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   Therefore for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a configuration of\n   \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required conditions exists.\n\n5. **Remaining case \\(k=n\\).**  \n   The construction above fails when \\(k=n\\) because it would require\n   \\(s=n-k=0\\) horizontal lines, leaving the whole set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) to be\n   covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines.  For odd \\(n\\) such a covering can be\n   produced (e.g. by taking the \\(n\\) lines \\(y = m x + c_m\\) with slopes\n   \\(m=1,2,\\dots ,n\\