nny\n   lines).  Exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and together they contain every\n   point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   Therefore for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a configuration of\n   \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required conditions exists.\n\n5. **Remaining case \\(k=n\\).**  \n   The construction above fails when \\(k=n\\) because it would require\n   \\(s=n-k=0\\) horizontal lines, leaving the whole set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) to be\n   covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines.  For odd \\(n\\) such a covering can be\n   produced (e.g. by taking the \\(n\\) lines \\(y = m x + c_m\\) with slopes\n   \\(m=1,2,\\dots ,n\\) and suitable intercepts), but for even \\(n\\) a\n   general construction has not been found.  Consequently the status of the\n   value \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\) remains open.\n\n**Conclusion.**  \nAll integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) are admissible; the case\n\\(k=n\\) is settled for odd \\(n\\) but not for even \\(n\\).  Hence the set of\nall admissible \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{\\,0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\}\\cup\\{n\\ \\text{(when $n$ is odd)}\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
