nts still uncovered are  \n> \\[\n> \\mathcal R_k=\n> \\Bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\;\\Big|\\;\n>          1\\le a\\le k,\\;\n>          s+1\\le b\\le n+1-a\n> \\Bigr\\}.\n> \\tag{4}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Verification:*  \nA point \\((a,b)\\) is uncovered precisely when \\(b>s\\) (otherwise a horizontal line would contain it) and still satisfies the original condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\). Moreover, if \\(a>k\\) then \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le s\\), contradicting \\(b>s\\). Hence the description (4) is accurate. Correct.\n\n#### 4.4 Size and shape of \\(\\mathcal R_k\\)\n\n> \u201cGeometrically \\(\\mathcal R_k\\) is a right\u2011isosceles lattice triangle of side\n> length \\(k\\); consequently  \n> \\[\n> |\\mathcal R_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n> \\tag{5}\n> \\]\u201d\n\n*Verification:*  \nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le k\\) the admissible \\(b\\) range has length \\((n+1-a)-(s+1)+1 = k-a+1\\). Summing over \\(a\\) yields \\(\\sum_{a=1}^{k}(k-a+1)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\). The geometric description is also accurate. Correct.\n\n#### 4.5 Sunny lines \\(S_j\\)\n\n> \u201cFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(0\\le j\\le k-1\\) define  \n> \\[\n> S_j:\\; y = (j+1)\\,x + \\bigl(s+1-(j+1)\\bigr).\n> \\tag{6}\n> \\]  \n> The slope of \\(S_j\\) equals \\(j+1\\); because \\(j+1\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\),\n> each \\(S_j\\) is sunny.\u201d\n\n*Verification:*  \nThe slope \\(j+1\\) is a positive integer, never \\(0\\), never undefined (\\(\\infty\\)), and never \\(-1\\). Hence each line is indeed sunny. Correct.\n\n#### 4.6 Claim that the \\(S_j\\) cover \\(\\mathcal R_k\\)\n\n> \u201cLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal R_k\\).  Since \\(1\\le a\\le k\\), write \\(j:=a-1\\)\n> (so \\(0\\le j\\le k-1\\)).  Substituting \\(j\\) into (6) we obtain  \n> \\[\n> y = a\\,x + (s+1-a).\n> \\tag{7}\n> \\]  \n> Evaluating (7) at \\(x=a\\) gives \\(y = a^2 + s+1-a\\).  Because\n> \\(b\\) satisfies \\(s+1\\le b\\le n+1-a = s+k+1-a\\), we have  \n> \\[\n> b = a + (s+1-a) = s+1,\n> \\]  \n> i.e. the point \\