ibility of \\(k=n\\)\n\n#### 4.1. Statement about the slope representation  \n\n> “For a sunny line the slope belongs to \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\). Write the slope as a reduced rational number \\(\\dfrac{p}{q}\\) (\\(p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1\\)). Any two distinct lattice points on that line differ by a vector \\((q,p)\\).”\n\n*Correct.*  \nAny line with rational slope can be written in reduced form; the minimal integer step between lattice points is indeed \\((q,p)\\). No gap.\n\n#### 4.2. Erroneous bound on points per line  \n\n> “Consequently the number of lattice points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that can lie on a single sunny line is at most  \n> \\[\n> 1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le 1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n> \\]  \n> (Indeed \\(\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}\\ge 2\\) because the forbidden slopes correspond to \\((p,q)=(0,1),(1,0),(1,-1)\\).)”\n\n*Critical Error.*  \nThe claim “\\(\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}\\ge 2\\)” is **false**. The allowed slope \\(1\\) has reduced representation \\((p,q)=(1,1)\\), giving \\(\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}=1\\). Consequently the inequality “\\(\\le 1+\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)” is not justified; for slopes with \\(\\max=1\\) the bound would be \\(1+\\lfloor n/1\\rfloor=n+1\\), which is larger. The step therefore contains a factual mistake and an unjustified inequality.\n\n#### 4.3. Consequence for the total number of covered points  \n\n> “Hence the total number of points that can be covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines is at most  \n> \\[\n> n\\Bigl(1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr)\n> \\;<\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge 3 .\n> \\]”\n\n*Critical Error (derived from the previous false bound).*  \nBecause the bound in the previous line is invalid, the derived estimate for the total number of covered points is not guaranteed. Moreover, the inequality is **false for \\(n=3\\)**: both sides equal \\(6\\). The text later acknowledges this equality, contradicting the claim of strict inequality for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n#### 4.4. Logical inconsistency  \n\n> “The inequality is a short elementary check: for \\(n=3\\) the left‑hand side equals \\(3\\cdot2=6\\) while \\(|\\mathcal P_3|=6\\); for \\(n\\ge4\\) the left‑hand side is strictly smaller.”\n\n*Critical Error.*  \nThe sentence first asserts a strict inequality for all \\(n\\ge3\\) and then admits equality at \\(n=3\\). This inconsistency undermines the logical chain.\n\n#### 4.5. Final conclusion  \n\n> “Therefore the whole set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) cannot be covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines, i.e. \\(k=n\\) is impossible for all \\(n\\ge3\\).”\n\n*Critical Error (false conclusion).*  \nA concrete counter‑example exists for \\(n=3\\):  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y = x,\\\\\nL_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,\\\\\nL_3 &: y = -2x + 5,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nall three are sunny and together cover the six points of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\). Hence the statement “\\(k=n\\) is impossible for all \\(n\\ge3\\)” is false.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Construction for every \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\n#### 5.1. Definition of \\(s\\)  \n\n> “Let \\(s:=n-k\\) (\\(s\\ge1\\)).”\n\n*Correct.*  \nSince \\(k\\le n-1\\), \\(s\\ge1\\). No gap.\n\n#### 5.2. Non‑sunny vertical lines  \n\n> “Use the \\(s\\) vertical lines \\(V_i:\\;x=i\\;(i=1,2,\\dots ,s).\\) … Every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(s\\) lies on one of these lines.”\n\n*Correct.*  \nVertical lines cover exactly the points with \\(a\\le s\\). No gap.\n\n#### 5.3. Description of the uncovered set  \n\n> “\\(\\mathcal R_k:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a\\ge s+1\\}.\\) … Because \\(a+b\\le n+1=s+k+1\\), the condition \\(a\\ge s+1\\) forces  \n> \\[\n> 1\\le b\\le k,\\qquad s+1\\le a\\le s+k+1-b .\n> \\]”\n\n*Correct.*  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le s+k+1\\) and \\(a\\ge s+1\\) we obtain \\(b\\le k\\); positivity gives \\(b\\ge1\\). The bounds on \\(a\\) follow directly. No gap.\n\n#### 5.4. Size of \\(\\mathcal R_k\\)  \n\n> “Geometrically \\(\\mathcal R_k\\) is a right‑isosceles lattice triangle of leg length \\(k\\); in particular \\(|\\mathcal R_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2.\\)”\n\n*Correct.*  \nSumming \\((k+1-b)\\) over \\(b=1,\\dots ,k\\) yields \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\). No gap.\n\n#### 5.5. Definition of sunny lines  \n\n> “For each integer \\(j\\) with \\(0\\le j\\le k-1\\) define the line  \n> \\[\n> S_j:\\; y = (j+1)\\,x - (j+1)s .\n> \\]”\n\n*Correct.*  \nThe slope is \\(j+1\\), which is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\), or \\(-1\\); thus each \\(S_j\\) is sunny. No gap.\n\n#### 5.6. Claim that the \\(S_j\\) cover \\(\\mathcal R_k\\)  \n\n> “**Claim.** Every point of \\(\\mathcal R_k\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(S_0,\\dots ,S_{k-1}\\).”\n\n*Critical Error.*  \nThe claim is false. Counter‑example: take \\(n=5,\\;k=2\\) (so \\(s=3\\)). Then \\(\\mathcal R_2=\\{(4,1),(4,2),(5,1)\\}\\). The two lines are  \n\n\\[\nS_0:\\; y = x-3,\\qquad S_1:\\; y = 2x-6 .\n\\]\n\nPoints \\((4,1)\\) and \\((4,2)\\) lie on \\(S_0\\) and \\(S_1\\) respectively, but \\((5,1)\\) lies on neither. Hence the claim fails.\n\n#### 5.7. Proof of the (false) claim  \n\n> “Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal R_k\\). … set \\(j:=b-1\\). … Substituting \\(j\\) into (11) gives \\(y = b\\,x - b\\,s\\). … Since \\(a\\ge s+1\\), we can write \\(a = s + t\\) with \\(t\\ge 1\\). … Plugging \\(x=a=s+t\\) into (12) yields \\(y = b\\,t\\). … therefore \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(S_{b-1}\\).”\n\n*Critical Error.*  \nThe algebraic manipulation is incorrect. Starting from the line equation \\(y = b\\,x - b\\,s\\) and substituting \\(x = s+t\\) gives \\(y = b(s+t) - b s = b t\\). Since the point under consideration has \\(y=b\\), the equality \\(b = b t\\) forces \\(t=1\\), i.e. \\(a = s+1\\). The proof mistakenly treats the derived equality \\(y = b t\\) as automatically satisfied for any \\(t\\ge1\\), ignoring the necessary condition \\(t=1\\). Consequently the argument does not establish that arbitrary \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge s+1\\) lies on the line; it only shows that points with \\(a=s+1\\) do. This is the precise reason the claim is false.\n\n#### 5.8. Assembling the whole family  \n\n> “The total collection consists of the \\(s=n-k\\) vertical lines (7) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines (11). … Hence the family satisfies the covering requirement and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.”\n\n*Critical Error (consequence of the previous false claim).*  \nSince the sunny lines do **not** cover all points of \\(\\mathcal R_k\\), the assembled set of \\(n\\) lines fails to cover the whole \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) for many values of \\(k\\). Therefore the construction does not prove existence for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 6. Overall conclusion  \n\n> “Combining the impossibility of \\(k=n\\) … with the explicit construction for all smaller \\(k\\) … we obtain the complete description of admissible values: \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).”\n\n*Critical Error.*  \nBoth pillars of the argument are flawed: the impossibility of \\(k=n\\) is false (counter‑example for \\(n=3\\)), and the construction for \\(k<n\\) does not work (fails already for \\(n=5,\\;k=2\\)). Hence the final answer is not established.\n\n--- \n\n**Summary of Errors**\n\n1. False statement that \\(\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}\\ge2\\) for any sunny line (Critical Error).  \n2. Unjustified bound \\(1+\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) for points per sunny line (Critical Error).  \n3. Incorrect inequality \\(n(1+\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor)<\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) claimed for all \\(n\\ge3\\) (Critical Error).  \n4. Contradictory text regarding the case \\(n=3\\) (Critical Error).  \n5. Wrong conclusion that \\(k=n\\) is impossible for all \\(n\\ge3\\) (Critical Error, contradicted by explicit example).  \n6. Claim that the lines \\(S_j\\) cover the whole set \\(\\mathcal R_k\\) is false (Critical Error).  \n7. Faulty algebraic proof of the above claim (Critical Error).  \n8. Final assembly relies on the false claim, so the construction does not provide the required configurations (Critical Error).  \n\nBecause of these critical flaws, the solution does **not** correctly solve the problem."
