            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points that must lie on at least one of the three lines.\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Sunny lines contain at most two points of \\(S\\)\n\nAssume a sunny line \\(\\ell\\) contains three distinct points of \\(S\\).  \nThose three points would be collinear with a slope not equal to \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nInspecting the six points of \\(S\\) one sees that any three collinear points among them lie on a vertical line (\\(x=1,2,3\\)), a horizontal line (\\(y=1,2,3\\)) or a line of slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=2,3,4\\)).  \nHence a sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(S\\).\n\nConsequently two sunny lines together can cover at most four points of \\(S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Existence of a non‑sunny line\n\nWe are asked for three distinct lines with exactly two sunny ones; therefore exactly one line, call it \\(\\ell\\), is non‑sunny.  \n\nIf \\(\\ell\\) contained **no** point of \\(S\\), the two sunny lines would have to cover all six points, contradicting the bound of four points from §1.  \nThus \\(\\ell\\) must pass through at least one point of \\(S\\).\n\nA non‑sunny line is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\); its equation is therefore one of\n\n\\[\nx=c,\\qquad y=c,\\qquad x+y=c,\n\\]\n\nwith a real constant \\(c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  How many points of \\(S\\) can lie on a non‑sunny line?\n\n*Vertical* (\\(x=c\\)):  \n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\nc & \\text{points of }S\\text{ on }x=c\\\\ \\hline\n1 & (1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\\\\n2 & (2,1),(2,2)\\\\\n3 & (3,1)\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n*Horizontal* (\\(y=c\\)):  \n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\nc & \\text{points of }S\\text{ on }y=c\\\\ \\hline\n1 & (1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\\\\n2 & (1,2),(2,2)\\\\\n3 & (1,3)\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n*Slope \\(-1\\)* (\\(x+y=c\\)):  \n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\nc & \\text{points of }S\\text{ on }x+y=c\\\\ \\hline\n2 & (1,1)\\\\\n3 & (1,2),(2,1)\\\\\n4 & (1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus any non‑sunny line contains **at most three** points of \\(S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Points not on the non‑sunny line\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT=S\\setminus\\ell .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|\\ell\\cap S|\\le 3\\), we have \\(|T|\\ge 3\\).  \nThe two sunny lines must together contain all points of \\(T\\).\n\nWe now examine every possible \\(\\ell\\) that meets \\(S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case A: \\(\\ell\\) is vertical.\n\n*Subcase A1:* \\(\\ell:x=1\\).  \n\\(T=\\{(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).  \nSlopes of the three possible pairs are \\(\\infty\\), \\(-1\\) and \\(-1\\); none is sunny.  \nHence no sunny line can contain two points of \\(T\\), contradicting the need for two sunny lines.\n\n*Subcase A2:* \\(\\ell:x=2\\).  \n\\(T=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\}\\).  \nEvery pair of points of \\(T\\) has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); again impossible.\n\n*Subcase A3:* \\(\\ell:x=3\\).  \n\\(T=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2)\\}\\).  \nAll six pairwise slopes belong to \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); thus no sunny line can contain two points of \\(T\\).  \n\nAll vertical possibilities lead to a contradiction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case B: \\(\\ell\\) is horizontal.\n\nThe analysis is symmetric to Case A (swap the roles of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\)).  \nFor \\(\\ell:y=1,2,3\\) the corresponding sets \\(T\\) are exactly the transposes of the sets in A1–A3, and the same slope calculations give only non‑sunny slopes.  \nHence no horizontal non‑sunny line works.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Case C: \\(\\ell\\) has slope \\(-1\\).\n\n*Subcase C1:* \\(\\ell:x+y=2\\).  \nThen \\(\\ell\\) contains only \\((1,1)\\) and \\(T=S\\setminus\\{(1,1)\\}\\) has five points.  \nAny two points of this five‑point set have slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore no sunny line can contain two of them – impossible.\n\n*Subcase C2:* \\(\\ell:x+y=3\\).  \n\\(\\ell\\) contains \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\); thus  \n\\(T=\\{(1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\\).\n\nThe six pairwise slopes are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(1,1)-(2,2):\\;1\\quad(\\text{sunny}),\\\\\n&(1,1)-(1,3):\\;\\infty,\\qquad (1,1)-(3,1):\\;0,\\\\\n&(1,3)-(2,2):\\;-1,\\qquad (1,3)-(3,1):\\;-1,\\qquad (2,2)-(3,1):\\;-1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus the only possible sunny line through points of \\(T\\) is the line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\).  \nThe remaining two points \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) lie on the line \\(x+y=4\\), which is non‑sunny.  \nConsequently we would have **two** non‑sunny lines (\\(x+y=3\\) and \\(x+y=4\\)), contradicting the requirement of exactly one.\n\n*Subcase C3:* \\(\\ell:x+y=4\\).  \n\\(\\ell\\) contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nT=S\\setminus\\ell=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe three pairwise slopes are \\(\\infty\\), \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); none is sunny.  \nThus the two sunny lines cannot cover the three points of \\(T\\), a contradiction.\n\nAll possibilities for a non‑sunny line lead to an impossibility.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nAssuming the existence of three distinct lines with exactly two sunny ones forces the existence of a non‑sunny line \\(\\ell\\) as above, but every admissible choice of \\(\\ell\\) contradicts the covering requirement. Therefore such a configuration does **not** exist for \\(n=3\\) and \\(k=2\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3,\\;k=2\\text{ is impossible.}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
