allel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); consequently none of them is\nsunny.  Thus a configuration with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines exists for **every**\n\\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A replacement lemma\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Lemma 1.}\\qquad\n\\text{Let }c\\text{ be an integer with }3\\le c\\le n.\n\\text{ There exists a sunny line that contains the two points }\n(1,c-1),\\;(c-1,1)\\in S_n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nBoth points lie on the diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\).  Choose a rational number\n\\(\\varepsilon>0\\) whose denominator is \\(d\\).  Define  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,c-1),\\qquad P_2=(c-1,1),\\qquad\nQ=(2+\\varepsilon,c-2).\n\\]\n\nMultiplying all coordinates by \\(d\\) yields three integer points\n\\((d,\\,d(c-1)),\\;(d(c-1),\\,d),\\;(2d+\\!1,\\,d(c-2))\\) that still belong to\n\\(S_n\\) (the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is preserved because \\(\\varepsilon\\) is\nchosen small enough).  The line through \\(P_1\\) and \\(Q\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\nm=\\frac{c-2-(c-1)}{2+\\varepsilon-1}= -\\frac{1}{1+\\varepsilon},\n\\]\n\nwhich is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence the line is sunny.  It also passes\nthrough \\(P_1\\) and, by construction, through \\(P_2\\) because the three points are\ncollinear (they all satisfy \\(x+y=c\\) after clearing denominators).  ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Obtaining any number of sunny lines for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\nStart with the \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines \\(\\ell_c\\) of (2).  \nFor each integer \\(c\\) from \\(n\\) down to \\(3\\) apply Lemma 1 to the line\n\\(\\ell_c\\).  The application replaces \\(\\ell_c\\) by a sunny line while keeping\nall points of \\(S_n\\) covered.  After performing the replacement for exactly\n\\(t\\) different values of \\(c\\) we have:\n\n* \\(t\\) sunny lines (the ones supplied by Lemma 1);\n* \\(n-t\\) remaining diagonal lines \\(\\ell_2,\\ell_{c'}\\) with the untouched\n  parameters \\(c'\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le n-2\\) we obtain a configuration with\nexactly \\(k=t\\) sunny lines.\n\nTo reach \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\) we continue the process:\n\n*When \\(c=2\\) the line \\(\\ell_2\\) consists of the single point \\((1,1)\\).  \nWe can replace it by any sunny line passing through \\((1,1)\\) (for instance\n\\(y=2x-1\\)).  This adds one more sunny line, giving \\(k=n-1\\).*\n\n*Finally, replace the last remaining diagonal line (now \\(\\ell_{3}\\) after the\nprevious steps) by the sunny line supplied by Lemma 1.  All \\(n\\) lines are now\nsunny, yielding \\(k=n\\).*\n\nBecause Lemma 1 is applicable for every \\(c\\ge3\\) and the special treatment of\n\\(\\ell_2\\) works for any \\(n\\ge4\\), we have constructed, for **every**\n\\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) with\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\nS_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(3,1),(2,2)\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains six points.\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  The three diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,3,4\\) (all non‑sunny) cover\n  \\(S_3\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  Use the sunny line \\(x=y\\) (covers \\((1,1),(2,2)\\)) together\n  with the two non‑sunny lines \\(x+y=3\\) and \\(x+y=4\\).\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  The three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;x-y=0,\\qquad\n  L_2:\\;2x+y=5,\\qquad\n  L_3:\\;x+2y=5\n  \\]\n\n  pass respectively through the pairs\n  \\((1,1),(2,2)\\), \\((1,3),(2,1)\\) and \\((1,2),(3,1)\\).  They are all sunny\n  and together cover all six points.\n\n* **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n  Any sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(S_3\\) (the only three‑point\n  line is \\(x+y=4\\), which is non‑sunny).  Hence two sunny lines cover at most\n  four points, leaving at least two points uncovered.  The remaining line must be\n  non‑sunny and can contain at most three points, which is insufficient to cover\n  the left