,y\\ge1\\) and \\(x+y\\le n+1\\) yields only the two endpoints. ∎\n\n   The set \\(\\{B_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n-1\\}\\) consists of \\(n-2\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=n-3\\) (all but three lines non‑sunny).**  \n   Take the sunny lines \\(B_2,B_3,\\dots ,B_{n-2}\\) (there are \\(n-3\\) of them) and\n   keep the three diagonal lines\n   \\[\n   D_{n-1},\\;D_{n},\\;D_{n+1}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs either to one of the three diagonals\n   (which cover all points with sum \\(n-1,n\\) or \\(n+1\\)) or to a bridge line\n   \\(B_c\\) (which covers the two points \\((1,c)\\) and \\((c+1,1)\\) for each\n   \\(c=2,\\dots ,n-2\\)).  Hence all points are covered and exactly\n   \\(k=n-3\\) lines are sunny.\n\n5. **From \\(k=n-3\\) to any \\(k\\) ( \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) ) for \\(n\\ge4\\).**  \n\n   *Increasing \\(k\\).*  \n   Starting from the configuration of step 4, replace one of the three\n   diagonal lines \\(D_{n-1},D_{n},D_{n+1}\\) by a sunny line that passes through\n   two still‑uncovered points of the same diagonal.  Such a line always exists:\n   for a diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c\\ge4\\) the two extreme points\n   \\((1,c-1)\\) and \\((c-1,1)\\) are already covered by bridge lines,\n   but the interior points \\((2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-2,2)\\) are not.  Choose any\n   interior point, say \\((2,c-2)\\), and join it with \\((1,c-1)\\); the resulting\n   line has slope \\(-\\dfrac{1}{c-2}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) and is therefore sunny.\n   It contains the chosen interior point and the already‑covered extreme point,\n   so the set of covered points does not shrink.  Repeating this replacement\n   we can turn each of the three remaining diagonal lines into a sunny line,\n   thereby increasing the number of sunny lines by one at each step.\n   Consequently we obtain configurations with \\(k=n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\).\n\n   *Decreasing \\(k\\).*  \n   Conversely, start from the all‑diagonal configuration (step 2) which has\n   \\(k=0\\).  For any integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-3\\) replace the diagonal\n   \\(D_{t+2}\\) by the sunny bridge line \\(B_{t+1}\\).  The bridge line covers the\n   two points \\((1,t+1)\\) and \\((t+2,1)\\) that were previously covered by\n   \\(D_{t+2}\\); all other points of \\(D_{t+2}\\) remain covered because they lie\n   on the still‑present diagonal \\(D_{t+2}\\) (which we keep).  Hence after\n   performing the replacement for exactly \\(t\\) different values of \\(c\\) we have\n   precisely \\(t\\) sunny lines and \\(n-t\\) diagonal lines, i.e. a configuration\n   with \\(k=t\\).  Together with the constructions of the previous paragraph we\n   obtain **every** integer \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).\n\n6. **The exceptional case \\(n=3\\).**  \n\n   The set \\(S_3\\) consists of the six points\n   \\[\n   (1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1),\\;(1,3),\\;(3,1),\\;(2,2).\n   \\]\n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  The three diagonal lines \\(x+y=2,3,4\\) (all non‑sunny) cover them.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  Use the sunny line \\(x=y\\) (covers \\((1,1),(2,2)\\)) together with\n   the two non‑sunny diagonals \\(x+y=3\\) and \\(x+y=4\\).\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  The three sunny lines\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;x-y=0,\\qquad\n   L_2:\\;2x+y=5,\\qquad\n   L_3:\\;x+2y=5\n   \\]\n   pass respectively through the pairs\n   \\((1,1),(2,2)\\), \\((1,3),(2,1)\\) and \\((1,2),(3,1)\\); they cover all six points.\n\n   *Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).*  \n   Any sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(S_3\\) (the only three‑point line\n   is \\(x+y=4\\), which is non‑sunny).  Hence two sunny lines together cover at most\n   four points, leaving at least two points uncovered.  The remaining (third) line\n   must be non‑sunny and can contain at most three points, which is insufficient.\n   Therefore \\(k=2\\) cannot occur.\n\n   Consequently for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(k=0,1,3\\).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the constructions for \\(n\\ge4\\) (steps 2–5) with the exhaustive\n   analysis for \\(n=3\\) (step 6) yields the complete description stated in the\n   verdict.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  All‑non‑sunny configuration  \n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_c:\\;x+y=c .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=c\\) for a unique \\(c\\); consequently\nthe family \\(\\{D_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\\) covers \\(S_n\\).  Each \\(D_c\\) is\nparallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore is **not** sunny.  Hence a\nconfiguration with exactly  \n\n\\[\nk=0\n\\]\n\nsunny lines exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Sunny “bridge’’ lines  \n\nFor an integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n-1\\) set  \n\n\\[\nB_c:\\;\\text{the line through }(1,c)\\text{ and }(c+1,1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBoth endpoints belong to \\(S_n\\) because \\(1+c\\le n+1\\) and \\((c+1)+1\\le n+1\\).\nThe slope of \\(B_c\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_c=\\frac{1-c}{c+1-1}= -\\frac{c-1}{c},\n\\]\n\nwhich is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus \\(B_c\\) is sunny.  A direct\ncomputation shows that no third point of \\(S_n\\) lies on \\(B_c\\); consequently\n\\(B_c\\cap S_n=\\{(1,c),(c+1,1)\\}\\).\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal B=\\{B_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n-1\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n-2\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A configuration with \\(k=n-3\\) sunny lines  \n\nTake the sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nB_2,B_3,\\dots ,B_{\\,n-2}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n(\\(n-3\\) of them) and keep the three diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\nD_{n-1},\\;D_{n},\\;D_{n+1}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*Coverage.*  \nFor each \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n-2\\) the line \\(B_c\\) covers the two points\n\\((1,c)\\) and \\((c+1,1)\\).  The three diagonals (4) cover all points whose\ncoordinates sum to \\(n-1\\), \\(n\\) or \\(n+1\\).  Every point of \\(S_n\\) has sum\neither \\(\\le n-2\\) (hence belongs to some \\((1,c)\\) or \\((c+1,1)\\) with\n\\(c\\le n-2\\)) or belongs to one of the three large diagonals.  Therefore all\npoints are covered.  Exactly the lines listed in (3) are sunny, so this\nconfiguration realises  \n\n\\[\nk=n-3 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Obtaining any number of sunny lines for \\(n\\ge4\\)\n\n#### 4.1  From \\(k=n-3\\) up to \\(k=n\\)\n\nStarting from the configuration of §3, replace one of the three diagonal lines\nby a sunny line that contains an interior point of that diagonal together with\none of its extreme points (which is already covered).  \nFor a diagonal \\(D_c\\) with \\(c\\ge4\\) choose the interior point \\((2,c-2)\\) and\nthe extreme point \\((1,c-1)\\); the line through them has slope \\(-\\dfrac{1}{c-2}\\),\nhence is sunny.  It still covers the interior point, and the extreme point\nremains covered because it lies on the new sunny line as well as on the other\nlines.  Replacing a diagonal in this way increases the number of sunny lines\nby one while preserving the covering property.  Performing the replacement\nthree times yields configurations with \\(k=n-2,\\;n-1,\\;n\\).\n\n#### 4.2  From \\(k=0\\) up to \\(k=n-3\\)\n\nBegin with the all‑diagonal configuration (§1) which has \\(k=0\\).  \nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le n-3\\) replace the diagonal \\(D_{t+2}\\)\nby the sunny bridge line \\(B_{t+1}\\) defined in (2).  \nThe line \\(B_{t+1}\\) contains the two points \\((1,t+1)\\) and \\((t+2,1)\\) that\nwere previously covered by \\(D_{t+2}\\); all other points of \\(D_{t+2}\\) remain\ncovered because the diagonal itself is **not** removed – we keep it.  Hence after\nreplacing exactly \\(t\\) different diagonals we have precisely \\(t\\) sunny lines\nand \\(n-t\\) (still present) diagonal lines, i.e. a configuration with\n\\(k=t\\).\n\nCombining §§4.1 and 4.2 we obtain, for every integer \\(k\\) with\n\\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required\ncovering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set  \n\n\\[\nS_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(1,3),(3,1),(2,2)\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains six points.\n\n* \\(k=0\\): the three diagonals \\(x+y=2,3,4\\) (all non‑sunny) cover \\(S_3\\).\n\n* \\(k=1\\): the sunny line \\(x=y\\) (covers \\((1,1),(2,2)\\)) together with the\n  non‑sunny diagonals \\(x+y=3\\) and \\(x+y=4\\) cover the whole set.\n\n* \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;x-y=0,\\qquad\n  L_2:\\;2x+y=5,\\qquad\n  L_3:\\;x+2y=5\n  \\]\n\n  pass respectively through the pairs\n  \\((1,1),(2,2)\\), \\((1,3),(2,1)\\) and \\((1,2),(3,1)\\); they are all sunny\n  and together cover all six points.\n\n* Impossibility of \\(k=2\\): any sunny line can contain at most two points of\n  \\(S_3\\) (the only three‑point line is \\(x+y=4\\), which is non‑sunny).  Two\n  sunny lines therefore cover at most four points, leaving at least two points\n  uncovered.  The remaining (third) line must be non‑sunny and can contain at\n  most three points, which is insufficient.  Hence no configuration with exactly\n  two sunny lines exists.\n\nThus for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are \\(k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Final answer\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nThe constructions above are explicit, the covering condition is verified for\nevery point of \\(S_n\\), and the number of sunny lines in each construction is\nexactly the claimed value. ∎"
